    title: "The Hertz Data Breach: How a Flaw in File-Transfer Software Exposed Renters' Driver's Licenses and Payment Cards",
    slug: "hertz-2025-cleo-data-breach-guide",
    author: "ScamShield Editorial",
    tags: ["guide","hertz-2025-data-breach"],
    body:
      `Hertz — which also owns and operates the Dollar and Thrifty rental brands — confirmed on February 10, 2025, that a hacker had stolen customer data during two intrusions the previous fall, on October 10 and December 25, 2024. The break-in didn't happen inside Hertz's own network at all: the attackers exploited a pair of "zero-day" vulnerabilities (flaws unknown to the software maker at the time, so no fix existed yet) in Cleo, a widely used file-transfer program that one of Hertz's vendors relied on to move data around. Cleo released a patch for the first flaw in October 2024, but researchers later found the fix was incomplete, leaving a second opening the attackers used weeks later — a gap Hertz itself had no visibility into or control over, since the software belonged to a vendor, not to Hertz.

The same Cleo vulnerabilities were exploited against dozens of other companies that fall, and cybersecurity researchers linked the campaign to Clop, a Russia-linked ransomware and extortion gang with a long track record of mass-hacking a single widely used piece of business software and then working through the list of every company it touched — the same approach the group used in 2023 against the file-transfer tool MOVEit, a breach that eventually hit thousands of organizations. Hertz initially told reporters in early 2025 it had "no evidence" its data was involved after Clop first listed it as a victim, then reversed course weeks later once its investigation confirmed the theft — a sequence that left some renters hearing conflicting information before the company's notification letters ever arrived that April.

What was exposed varied from person to person, but Hertz said the stolen data could include names, contact information, dates of birth, driver's license numbers, payment card information, and — for a smaller group of people, generally those who had filed an injury or workers' compensation claim connected to a rental — Social Security numbers, passport numbers, or Medicare and Medicaid ID numbers. Hertz has not published a total nationwide figure for how many customers were affected, but state attorney general filings gave a sense of scale: more than 3,400 residents notified in Maine alone and nearly 97,000 in Texas, figures that, extrapolated across all 50 states plus Hertz's international customer base in the UK, EU, Canada, and Australia, point to a breach touching a substantial share of the tens of millions of people who rent from Hertz, Dollar, or Thrifty every year.

For renters, the Hertz breach is a reminder that handing your driver's license and credit card to a company at the counter doesn't mean that data stays only with that company — it often flows to background-processing vendors, in this case a file-transfer tool most customers have never heard of, and any one of those vendors can become the actual point of failure. If you've rented from Hertz, Dollar, or Thrifty in the past several years, don't assume you're in the clear just because you never received a letter — notifications went out gradually and varied by state, and some mailing addresses on file are years out of date. Hertz is offering affected customers free credit monitoring and identity-protection services through Kroll; enroll using the activation code from your own notification letter, not a link in an unsolicited email, since large breaches like this one reliably draw copycat phishing messages within weeks. Because driver's license numbers and, for some victims, Social Security numbers were exposed, placing a free credit freeze with Equifax, Experian, and TransUnion is worth doing whether or not a letter ever arrives, and anyone whose driver's license number was compromised should also consider contacting their state's Department of Motor Vehicles to ask about a replacement. Report any suspected identity theft at IdentityTheft.gov, and any phishing message referencing the Hertz breach to ReportFraud.ftc.gov.`,
    sourceUrl: "https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/hertz-confirms-customer-info-drivers-licenses-stolen-in-data-breach/",
